Krypton Wrapper (<=26.2, formerly NG-GL4ES) is the gl4es fork used by ZalithLauncher 2: it translates desktop OpenGL to OpenGL ES, with shaders converted through a glslang (GLSL to SPIR-V) + SPIRV-Cross (SPIR-V to ESSL) pipeline -- a different road from VGPU's built-in converter.

When to pick it:
• legacy versions (1.8.9-era Forge) showing corrupted textures, broken atlases or stripes under VGPU (two fix rounds did not root that out; it is a translation-layer defect) -- switching to Krypton Wrapper is the first countermeasure;
• upstream claims almost every MC version runs, including 26.x (fall back to Zink if not);
• advanced tuning can go into config.json under ngg/ in the game directory (rarely needed).

Where: Settings -> Video -> Renderer -> Krypton Wrapper (<=26.2) (only changeable while the game is not running).

Yes (a known trait). MobileGlues is an OpenGL → Metal translation layer, and the new 26.x chunk pipeline pushes it hard: at the same render distance the translation call volume is roughly 3x the older versions', so the framerate dips noticeably while moving. Android devices running MobileGlues stutter the same way — it's an inherent cost of the upstream translation stack.

Mitigations, ranked by value:
1. Switch to the Vulkan-direct backend (MobileGL) and enable an FSR tier (the recommended path since Task166 — smoothest during chunk loading);
2. Switch to the Zink renderer (chunk streaming throughput is clearly higher);
3. Enable FSR upscaling to lower the render resolution (see the next entry);
4. Keep render distance around 10 — raising it only stretches out the loading storm.

Ruled out: GC, RAM size, and GPU performance are NOT the cause — simply raising the memory allocation will not cure it.

FSR 1.0 is enabled under Settings → Video Settings → FSR 1.0 Upscaling (Ultra Quality 77% / Quality 67% / Balanced 59% / Performance 50%). When enabled, the game renders at a lower resolution and FSR's EASU kernel scales it back up to full screen: clearly higher framerates with a slight quality loss.

Supported renderers (multi-renderer support is recent):
• MobileGlues: FSR1 built in (recommended, most mature). The mg renderer's GLES backend and OpenGL 4.0 backend are this renderer (same path since Task158 — tier changes take effect directly);
• Zink: the same EASU upscaling kernel, applied right before presentation. (Early builds had two display bugs on Zink — a green/garbled screen and a "split picture" — both fixed: the first was GLSL 4.1 version adaptation plus half-precision function completion, the second was the upscaling/readback execution order being inverted; it now upscales before readback with the default framebuffer explicitly bound, a closed loop);
• mg's Vulkan-direct backend (MobileGL): supported (since Task166/167, via a Metal presentation-layer interception of the upscale; full FSR1 = EASU+RCAS, verified on device);
• Other renderers (MoltenVK / Auto / gl4es etc.): not supported yet.

Notes:
1. Keep the resolution slider at 100% — no need to lower it manually (that would just double-scale);
2. The historical "black screen after enabling", "picture shrunk into the bottom-left corner", and "green garbage on Zink" bugs were each fixed (shader downgrade fix + render-viewport detection fix + shader version adaptation and half-precision completion). If you still see them, upload a log;
3. FSR's upscale happens once per frame after the game image is done and costs very little. If it feels like "FSR makes it slower", it's almost certainly chunk-loading stutter (see the MobileGlues entry) and unrelated to FSR.

• Framerate cap: in-game Video Settings → Max Framerate (the -Dmax.fps unlock layer is also visible in the launcher's Java arguments). Set it to "Unlimited" or a high value to lean on the cap;
• VSync: after turning off in-game Video Settings → VSync, every renderer's presentation mode switches to immediate present (no longer waiting for refresh) — big gains on high-refresh displays;
• 26.x modpacks stuck at 30fps while loading / idle (once mis-blamed on FSR): MC 26.3 added an "inactivity framerate limiter" — in the default AFK mode, 60s without input drops to 30fps (10 minutes drops to 10fps), and a modpack's touch-free loading period triggers exactly that. Fixed by the launcher (Task104): writes inactivityFpsLimit=minimized plus a zero-delta scroll heartbeat every 45s to reset the timer; log anchor [InputDiag] Task104 AFK heartbeat. If heavy play still sits at ~30fps after the fix, lower the render distance first (heavy packs: ≤16 recommended) and drop the FSR tier;
• 26.x Zink+FSR pinned at a stable 29–30fps during play (Task104/106 adjudication): measured moving FSR from 1.3x to 2.0x (−58% rendered pixels) moved the framerate not at all — the bottleneck isn't game render load but the per-frame presentation constant of two full-frame GPU→CPU readbacks (driver readback + authoritative readback) plus a row-flip copy. The launcher optimized this (Task106 bundle-direct): once the twin sentinels prove frame-over-frame that the driver buffer already holds the full current frame, it skips the duplicate readback and row flip and presents the driver buffer directly; log anchor [OSMBridge] Task106 bundle-direct present engaged. The heartbeat line gained phase timings to self-certify the bottleneck split: t=swap (launcher total), [pre+easu / glFinish / readback] (phases), frame (full frame period), MC-side = frame − swap (game's own cost) — if MC-side dominates, only render distance/renderer changes help; if glFinish/readback dominates, please attach the log (next round keeps compressing the presentation constant);
• "Modpack caps at 30fps but vanilla is fine" (Task110 root cause): the dynamic-fps mod (bundled in many packs, e.g. 3.11.10 here) throttles whenever it sees an "unfocused window" (vanilla doesn't ship it, hence fine; the game itself always judges focused). Fixed at the root (Task110): the SDL window flags now always report "focused + visible" (in embedded mode the game view IS the frontmost focus; iOS freezes rendering in background anyway, so no side effects), and the mod's state machine settles in the normal "focused, uncapped" gear. Log anchor [SDLHook] Task110. Other background-throttling mods (IdleHandler-style) are immunized the same way;
• When the framerate still won't climb, split the bottleneck first: GPU saturated (lower render distance / enable FSR) versus chunk loading (switch to Zink / lower render distance);
• Wildly fluctuating but decent average framerate: usually a chunk streaming storm — see the MobileGlues stutter entry.

Common causes and fixes:

1. FSR tier too low (Balanced/Performance): try "Ultra Quality" or toggle FSR off to compare;
2. The resolution slider was manually lowered: restore 100% (prefer FSR tiers for resolution reduction — much better quality);
3. The historical 1x-pinned blur was fixed (render surface at 1:1 with physical pixels); if it reappears, please report;
4. UI scale set too small: raise in-game Video Settings → GUI Scale;
5. The sodium-extra modpack option "Reduce resolution on Mac": packs bundling sodium-extra (e.g. BMC2) running under the Mac-spoofed environment, when this option is on (Video Settings → sodium-extra settings → Performance → Reduce resolution on Mac), halve the framebuffer then upscale it back (measured 590x410 rendered up to 2360x1640 — an effective 4x scale = clearly soft). This is the mod's own setting, not a launcher issue — turn it off in the mod's settings to restore full-resolution rendering; if you're chasing framerate keep it or switch to an FSR tier instead (better quality).

Tip: to tell "blurry" from "low resolution" — zoom into a screenshot and look at block edges: jagged = resolution; hazy = filtering/scaling.

Yes, but mind version pairing (the community's #1 crash cause):

1. Iris and Sodium versions are hard-linked: Iris pins an exact Sodium version — updating only one yields "Some of your mods are incompatible". Update both together, or use the launcher-recommended versions;
2. The 26.x Sodium line has further dependencies (Sodium Options API etc.); when errors mention dependency conflicts, add/align versions as prompted;
3. OptiFine is mutually exclusive with Sodium/Iris — never install them together;
4. Shaders are heavy on the GPU: test with low/medium tiers first, then push higher; with shaders on, Zink + FSR is the recommended combo;
5. Crash after installing mods: read the "Caused by" section of the crash report (viewable in the in-game menu) — nine times out of ten it's a version mismatch, not the launcher.

Usage first: open a text field before typing (tap the chat button or press T to open chat), then your input goes into the field.

Two keyboards, two buttons, different jobs:

[System keyboard] the ✎ icon ("Input Method" button)
Brings up the iOS system soft keyboard with Chinese input methods, predictive text, and emoji. Ways to trigger:
1. Tap the "✎ Input Method" button on your control layout (tap again to dismiss);
2. Or long-press the screen with two fingers (enable first under Settings → Controls → Two-finger keyboard summon).

[Button keyboard] the ⸰ icon ("Keyboard" drawer)
Expands into a full key panel (QWERTY + symbols + F keys); letters go straight into the game without the system keyboard:
• Hold SHIFT then a letter for uppercase; the "Caps Lock" button toggles case state;
• Symbol keys respect SHIFT (e.g. SHIFT+, gives <);
• With Ctrl/Alt held, letters act as shortcuts and don't enter text (same as a real keyboard).

Troubleshooting:
1. Letters do nothing: confirm the chat field is actually open (T key or chat button);
2. Early builds had the ⸰ panel completely unresponsive (the panel's background plate swallowed touches — fixed). If an old install still misbehaves on first launch after upgrading, use "Restore default controls" in the keybind editor to fetch the latest factory layout;
3. A specific key behaves wrong (e.g. symbols misplaced): a custom layout may have changed it — restore defaults;
4. Still broken: upload a log (it records every button trigger).

• Bluetooth mouse: connect/switch it on before launching the game. A mouse enabled mid-game is occasionally not recognized (a known upstream issue) — exiting and re-entering the game fixes it;
• Pointer lock: after entering the game and grabbing the view, the pointer locks automatically; there's a "hide hardware pointer" option in Settings as needed;
• Gamepad: Xbox layout supported (switchable under Settings → Controls → Gamepad type); sticks/triggers/shoulders all have default mappings and can be rebound;
• Pad drift / not detected: first confirm the pad itself is fine in system settings, then check whether in-game key binds were wiped.

As a rule you can allocate up to ~50% of total device RAM (e.g. 3GB on an 8GB device). With the increased-memory-limit entitlement, up to ~65%; with the extended-virtual-addressing entitlement, up to ~75%.

Other value-per-effort notes:
• Render distance 12 is the sweet spot — beyond that you mainly lengthen loading storms and raise memory use;
• Render resolution: prefer FSR tiers over manually dragging the resolution slider;
• When things lag, first split "chunk loading lag" (switch to Zink / enable FSR / lower render distance) from "generally low framerate" (lower render distance / resolution / shaders) and treat accordingly.

Newer builds auto-fetch it: when a modpack install finds the parent version JSON missing or corrupt, the launcher pulls it automatically from both Mojang's official source and the BMCLAPI mirror — normally no need to pre-install vanilla manually.

If it still fails:
1. Check the network (it only errors when both sources are unreachable);
2. Or install the matching vanilla version manually from the Downloads page first, then the modpack;
3. Fully quit and reopen the launcher before retrying.

Install: drop the .jar into the mods folder of the matching version (with version isolation on: instance dir/versions/<version>/mods). Fabric mods need Fabric Loader installed first; same for Forge.

Crash triage order:
1. Read the "Caused by" and "Mixin apply failed" sections of the crash report — the vast majority are inter-mod version conflicts or MC-version mismatches;
2. Performance mods (Sodium/Iris/Lithium etc.) conflict frequently with each other and with other rendering mods — bisect after each addition;
3. Two mods serving the same function: pick one (two minimaps or two optimization packs cannot coexist);
4. Only suspect the launcher after removing everything and still crashing — then please attach the log.

The launcher auto-selects the correct Java runtime per game version — normally no manual action needed:

• MC 26.x → Java 25;
• MC 1.20.5 – 1.21.x → Java 21;
• MC 1.17 – 1.20.4 → Java 17;
• MC 1.16.5 and earlier → Java 8.

A modpack profile's declared javaVersion is auto-corrected by the launcher (values old packs got wrong no longer block launch). If a manual Forge install complains about the Java version, check whether the launcher's Java runtime setting was changed.

Everything lives under the app sandbox's Documents directory (POJAV_HOME):

• versions/: game versions plus their isolated mods/configs;
• saves/: your worlds;
• controlmap/: custom control layouts (default.json is the default layout, editable via Settings → Keybind Editor);
• MG/: MobileGlues renderer config (config.json, including the FSR tier).

Backup: copy the whole Documents directory out via the Files app or the sidebar's file manager entry; on a new device/reinstall, copy it back to the same place. Uninstalling the launcher wipes the sandbox — back up important saves first.

The log latestlog.txt is also here — grab it when reporting issues.

The launcher uses both Mojang's official source and the BMCLAPI mirror, auto-switching and retrying on failure. Still slow/failing:

1. Confirm the network can reach at least one of piston-meta.mojang.com or bmclapi2.bangbang93.com;
2. Check the download-source setting (the preferred source is switchable in Settings); on mainland-China networks prefer BMCLAPI;
3. Version list failing to load: fully quit and reopen the launcher (the remote list is re-fetched);
4. Large files (asset packs/modpacks) interrupted: tapping download again resumes/retries — no need to wipe data.

This is iOS restricting background GPU privileges, most visible on the Zink (Vulkan) path: when the app goes to the background, the system reclaims GPU submission rights immediately — if the render thread is still submitting work, the Vulkan device is permanently lost (log signature: VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST / zink: DEVICE LOST). Back in the foreground the picture is frozen and touch is dead.

State of play and advice:
1. Avoid backgrounding mid-game (split-screen peeks at notifications/Control Center are usually fine; a full switch is what triggers it);
2. Already frozen: exit and re-enter the game (a lost Vulkan device cannot be recovered);
3. If you frequently need to switch away (checking guides): use the MobileGlues renderer (the Metal path is more forgiving of background switches) or check guides on another device;
4. Brief garbled frame after returning but playable: residual frame queue — heals by itself within seconds.

Note: this is not low memory and not the launcher killing the process — more memory allocation will not help. Automatic recovery from device loss requires rebuilding the Vulkan device at a lower level; it's on the roadmap.

The most valuable artifact is the log file latestlog.txt (in the launcher sandbox's Documents directory, also viewable via the in-game menu): it's kept automatically after every game session. Attach it together with:

1. Device model and OS version (e.g. iPad Air M4 / iPadOS 26.6);
2. Game version and mod list (e.g. 26.3 + Sodium);
3. Renderer and FSR settings;
4. When the problem happens (entering a world / mid-play / after pausing).

Most issues can be pinned down from the log alone — no screen recording needed. Classic cases located from logs alone: a render-race crash at world load, chunk-pipeline translation overhead, Sodium transparency-sorting artifacts, and more.

Try in order (retry after each step):

1. Fully quit the launcher (swipe it away) and reopen — clears most transient states;
2. Try a different renderer (Zink ↔ MobileGlues) — separates renderer issues from environment issues;
3. Turn FSR off / lower the resolution — rules out the upscale path;
4. Create a clean save / no-mods version and enter — rules out saves and mods;
5. Still broken: attach latestlog.txt (previous entry) and mention which step you reached and which screen you were stuck on.

Don't reflexively "wipe all data" — most issues have nothing to do with data; wiping loses your saves and fixes nothing.

Typical picture: the launch overlay / progress screen sits still for minutes, but the game doesn't crash. This is unrelated to renderer and memory allocation — more memory does nothing.

Confirmed case (BMC2 pack, 537 mods): the pack bundles a "desktop dialog" utility mod (missingmodschecker — on desktop it pops a dialog when dependencies are missing and waits for confirmation). Such dialogs can never appear on iOS, so the main thread waits forever. The log's [LaunchWatchdog] lines name the mod it's stuck on directly.

The launcher has two layers of protection built in:
1. Before launch, known dialog-class mods are auto-disabled (look for [ModDialogGuard] in the log — the mod file was renamed to .disabled; rename it back to .jar to restore);
2. Launch watchdog: when the main thread makes no progress for 15+ seconds, latestlog.txt automatically records where execution is; lines starting with [LaunchWatchdog] — the "at <mod package>.<Class>" line points at the guilty mod.

Self-help:
1. Wait patiently for 2–3 minutes first — some mods are genuinely slow on first init;
2. Cancel and retry once — the first launch copies the pack's default files; the second skips that work;
3. Still stuck: check the mod [LaunchWatchdog] names, remove it from the mods folder (utility mods are usually safe to remove), retry;
4. If none of it makes sense: upload latestlog.txt — the watchdog record pinpoints it directly.

Typical picture: the mod list prints, launch progresses for ~20–30 seconds, then a sudden exit(-1); switching renderers does nothing. Searching latestlog.txt for "Could not execute entrypoint stage" shows a NoClassDefFoundError / ClassNotFoundException chain underneath — this is not a renderer or memory problem, it's missing mods in the mods folder: a jar that some installed mod references (provided by FTB Library, Balm, TerraBlender, etc.) simply isn't there.

Confirmed case (BMC2, 536 mods): during import, 8+ files from the FTB family + Balm + TerraBlender failed to download or were skipped; earlier in the log a "Dependencies overridden for ..." line (from config/fabric-loader.json's dependencyOverrides) had also silenced Fabric's missing-dependency errors, so the gap lurked until the entrypoint stage blew up.

The launcher has three layers of protection:
1. At the end of an import, the failed/skipped list is written to import_report.json in the instance directory;
2. On the next launch, unacknowledged gaps trigger a one-time reminder (search the log for [ImportGuard] Task95);
3. If it does crash, the crash screen lists the missing classes and the inferred component names (FTB Quests/Balm etc.).

Self-help:
1. Per the crash screen / reminder, re-import the instance fresh (or use a different download source), or manually add the named components in the mod manager (versions must match the pack's requirements);
2. If the log has "Dependencies overridden for ...": after fixing, you can delete the dependencyOverrides entry in config/fabric-loader.json so missing dependencies fail fast again;
3. When sharing latestlog.txt, attach import_report.json (instance root) so the missing files can be reconciled directly.
